## Summary

This paper contributes a theoretical framework analyzing physics-informed kernel methods for linear inverse problems, specifically showing that PDE operators enable benign overfitting in fixed dimensions and that optimal convergence requires a specific smoothness threshold for the inductive bias. The single most important piece of evidence is **Theorem 4.2**, which bounds the variance of the minimum norm interpolator by $\tilde{O}(n^{\max\{2p+\lambda\beta', -1\}})$, proving that the negative spectral decay $p$ inherent to PDE operators stabilizes the variance term sufficiently to prevent catastrophic overfitting even without high-dimensional scaling.

